=== BR-AR-0019 REOPENING (fourth pass): TESTS (c), the test-registry shape, recognised at ANY nesting depth and
    resolved against real code-layer test symbols / the id grammar ===

--- Mechanism ---
govbridge/graph/derive.py: _iter_registry_rows walks the WHOLE parsed YAML document (every dict, every list
element, at every depth) and yields any mapping whose OWN "tests" key is a list of strings -- never a top-level-
or rows/entries-only check. _registry_row_identifier: the first of REGISTRY_ROW_ID_KEYS=("id","item") present,
else the row's own containing key-path + index (never a guess). _resolve_registry_test_entry resolves each entry
in three buckets: (1) a Rust a::b::fn-shaped candidate (_rust_path_candidate: the entry's own TRAILING
whitespace-separated token, generic enough to read through a free-text prefix like "unit ...") against
{qualified_name: count} for every is_test=1 code_symbol reachable at the ref's own commit
(_test_symbol_counts_for_ref, a JOIN against the ALREADY-SHARED conn's own "occurrence" table -- never a second
sqlite connection to the store, see the fixed isolation bug below); EXACT_TEST_REGISTRY_ROW on a unique full-string
match, HEURISTIC_TEST_REGISTRY_SYMBOL otherwise (not found, ambiguous, or resolved only by the candidate's own
trailing segment since this repository's own tree-sitter adapter gives a bare/mod-nested function's qualified_name
no module prefix at all -- rust_treesitter.py's own _walk); (2) failing that, the id grammar's mention shape ->
HEURISTIC_TEST_REGISTRY_ID_TOKEN; (3) neither -> HEURISTIC_TEST_REGISTRY_RAW_TEXT, ALWAYS counted, never dropped.
test_registry_edges_for_id's own git-grep is no longer restricted to tests/.

--- A real isolation bug found and fixed before this reopening's return ---
The first working version of requirement 3 resolved code symbols via govbridge.code.symbols.ensure_indexed
through its OWN _open_conn() -- a SEPARATE sqlite3 connection to whatever GOVBRIDGE_STORE currently names,
setting PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL on it as a side effect of opening. Called during ANY "lineage" build (every ref,
every build), this silently broke tests/core/test_freshness.py::
test_two_govbridge_store_values_build_independent_stores's own byte-for-byte "rebuilding store A never touches
store B's file" guarantee (a store's file-change-counter bumps on a redundant, no-op open_db() call too).
Confirmed by isolating the change: reverting lineage_layer.py to its pre-fourth-reopening state makes the test
pass again; the current lineage_layer.py without the fix reproduces the failure standalone. Fixed by NEVER
opening a second connection: _test_symbol_counts_for_ref reads code_symbol via a JOIN on the SAME, already-open
conn every build already shares, through the "core" layer's own occurrence table (always populated first,
structurally -- see the function's own comment for why). Verified: tests/core/test_freshness.py passes standalone
and in the full suite; the new registry/code-symbol test itself now sets up code_symbol on the SAME in-memory
connection first (core_layer_builder then code_layer_builder then lineage_layer_builder), exactly mirroring a
real freshness.run()'s own layer order, never a second connection anywhere.

